is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The firewall (iptables/netfilter) on router6, specifically the FORWARD chain.

Mechanism: Traffic from the rest of the campus toward the “other group” subnets behind router6 arrives on router6’s upstream interface eth2 (10.50.1.2). Router6 has explicit DROP rules on the FORWARD chain for any packets with in-interface eth2, allowing only one narrow exception. As a result, ICMP and all non-exempt traffic from your group are dropped at router6 and never reach the 172.18.51.0/24 hosts, making it look like you’re “banned.”

Localization: Router6 (interfaces eth2 -> eth0/eth1). Evidence:
- Your group hosts (e.g., laptop0 172.16.20.10) can ping 192.168.200.11 but time out to 172.18.51.15/16/17/18. Router0 and PCs in other VLANs also time out to 172.18.51.15.
- Hosts inside the “other group” segment can reach each other: laptop4 (172.18.50.14) and router6 can ping 172.18.51.15 successfully.
- Router6 iptables shows:
  -A FORWARD -s 172.16.10.0/24 -d 172.18.51.1/32 -i eth2 -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
Counters show packets being dropped on these rules.

Root cause: Misconfigured/overly restrictive firewall on router6 dropping all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, with only a narrow HTTP exception.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove or adjust the FORWARD-chain DROP rules for -i eth2. At minimum, add ACCEPT rules to permit required inter-VLAN traffic (e.g., -A FORWARD -i eth2 -d 172.18.50.0/24 -j ACCEPT and -A FORWARD -i eth2 -d 172.18.51.0/24 -j ACCEPT, including ICMP), or flush the FORWARD chain to restore default allow if appropriate. Ensure policy aligns with intended inter-group access. === END ===